# CI, checks and the lint gate

## Don't manually trigger CI runs

Do not manually trigger or re-run CI workflows (a manual dispatch, a re-run of a failed run) unless the owner explicitly says to.
CI minutes are expensive, and the Main Health thread already watches `main` and reports a red build.
Every check workflow under `.github/workflows/` is triggered by manual dispatch only.

Two exceptions belong to the Main Health thread alone, and cover no other agent and no extra run:

- **The scheduled full pass.** The owner's standing schedule has it dispatch every check workflow on `main` (twice a day by default), only if something has merged since the last pass.
- **A single re-run** when a failure looks infrastructural or possibly flaky.

The gap between passes has no real-CI signal, so `{{ local_full_suite_script }}` is this project's own local stand-in for it: project-owned, not generated, and its purpose, schedule and evidence format (`watch-main-ci`'s Routine 2) are a stage 0 task, not something left for Main Health to notice is missing.
When the stand-in's name or publish switch differs from the defaults, stage 0 sets the `local_full_suite_script`/`local_full_suite_publish_var` answers the same way as `coverage_floor` below (`copier update --trust --defaults --skip-tasks --vcs-ref <_commit> --data local_full_suite_script=<path> --data local_full_suite_publish_var=<VAR>`), rather than hand-editing `watch-main-ci`'s table row.

## A ticket's own checks (Default)

An implementation ticket's standing merge gate: run the test files near the change, keep coverage of the touched modules from dropping, clear the review gate (`.forge/review-gate.md`), and self-merge.
Never write a ticket's "done" criteria as "dispatch CI", "link a green run", "run the full suite", or "wait on a review beyond the standing gate": those spend CI minutes or human time budgeted only for Main Health's scheduled passes and the standing review gate.
A specific brief or the owner can ask for a real CI run or extra review on a specific ticket when the work genuinely needs it, such as a migration only a real database can validate, but only explicitly on that ticket.
`to-tickets` and `triage` phrase checks as "run the tests near the change; coverage of touched modules must not drop".

## The coverage floor is part of finishing a PR

The main test suite has a coverage floor ({{ coverage_floor }}%, the `coverage_floor` answer in `.copier-answers.yml`, which stage 0 sets from a measured baseline), enforced by the suite's own configuration whenever the full suite runs.
The floor only ratchets up: raising it needs no approval, lowering it needs the owner's.
A change to the floor changes the suite's configuration and that answer in the same PR (`copier update --trust --defaults --skip-tasks --vcs-ref <_commit> --data coverage_floor=<n>`, with `_commit` from `.copier-answers.yml` so the TheForge version does not move).
`scripts/test_coverage_floor_answer.py` holds this sentence's percentage equal to the answer, as `scripts/test_check_module_size.py` holds the size caps; the suite's configuration file is stage 0's choice and not in the template, so keeping that third value in step stays a PR-process duty, not a mechanical check.
`--defaults` is required non-interactively: without it Copier re-asks every other question and, with no TTY, dies with `EOFError`; with it, every other answer keeps its value from `.copier-answers.yml`.
The full suite runs on Main Health's schedule, not per PR, so a PR protects coverage locally: one that adds code with real decisions (branches, error handling, retries, state transitions) tests those paths itself and checks coverage of the modules it touched before merging.
A coverage regression on `main` is a gate outage for everyone else's PRs, not a minor clean-up.

## Lint is enforced by the pre-commit hook, not CI

The git pre-commit hook (`.pre-commit-config.yaml`) is the real lint gate.
Install it once per clone with `pre-commit install --install-hooks` (the session-start hook does this where the harness runs it), and check it is installed (`test -f .git/hooks/pre-commit`) before assuming it ran.
Never commit with `--no-verify` to get past it: fix what it flags.

The project's own commands, set at stage 0:

- setup: `{{ setup_command }}`
- full test suite: `{{ test_command }}`
- lint and format check: `{{ lint_command }}`

## Operational constants

`docs/agents/operational-constants.md` inventories the project's limits, intervals and tuning values, saying which are deploy-time configuration and which are deliberately hardcoded invariants; a PR that adds or changes one updates it in the same PR.

## Deployment configuration lives in the repo (Never)

The project's deployment and environment configuration (configuration defaults and templates, images, the devcontainer, workflows) lives in this repo.
No agent changes a running deployment's configuration in place; any change is a ticketed PR to the repo's own file, redeployed from there.
